"A phone number in Taiwan usually has ten digits.": "961e7bc68e6b",
"Each 0 or 1 is called a bit, which is short for binary digit. The American scientist John Tukey first used the word in 1947, and Claude Shannon made it famous in 1948. In 1970, a memory chip from Intel that held 1,024 bits began to replace older kinds of computer memory. Today a phone with 128 gigabytes of storage holds about one trillion bits. Taiwan helps make memory, too: companies such as Nanya Technology, Winbond, and Macronix produce memory chips.": "84cc96b35db8",
"Each student has a code to log in to the computer.": "c8c39c8d0b2c",
"How can 0s and 1s make words? Computers group bits in eights, and eight bits are called a byte. Eight bits can make 256 different patterns, enough to give every English letter its own number. The letter A is number 65, which in bits is 01000001. Most Chinese characters need three bytes, or 24 bits. Photos work the same way: each tiny dot, or pixel, usually uses 8 bits each for red, green, and blue. A 12-megapixel photo is about 288 million bits before the phone squeezes it into a smaller file.": "088061e0a899",
