"So how does your phone keep your photos? It also has flash memory, invented by Fujio Masuoka at Toshiba in Japan in 1980. In flash memory, electrons are pushed into a tiny box surrounded by insulating material, so they stay there even without power. A computer uses fast RAM for the work it is doing right now and flash memory to keep your files for later. So save your work often, and keep a backup copy of the photos you love.": "7192f7da7de5",
"There is a problem. The charge in each cell slowly leaks away, like water from a cup with a tiny hole. So the computer refreshes its memory: it reads every cell and tops up the 1s, usually every 64 milliseconds. When you turn the power off, nothing refreshes the cells, and the 1s fade into 0s. That is why RAM forgets everything when a computer shuts down, and why work you have not saved can be lost.": "7d71c118b536",
