"Where are the bits kept? In a computer's working memory, called RAM, each bit sits in a microscopic cell made of a tiny switch, called a transistor, and a tiny store of electric charge, called a capacitor. A charged cell means 1, and an empty cell means 0. Billions of cells are lined up in rows, and every byte has its own number, called an address, so the computer can find any byte in an instant.": "4f7612076ab7",
